{"claims": [
  {
    "claim_text": "Older versions of SQLite needed write access just to read a WAL-mode database.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [4] explicitly states that older SQLite versions could not read a WAL-mode database that was read-only and that write access was required to read it.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 4,
        "quote": "Older versions of SQLite could not read a WAL-mode database that was read-only. In other words, write access was required in order to read a WAL-mode database."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "That restriction was lifted in version 3.22.0, so current versions can read a WAL database on read-only media as long as certain conditions are met.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [4] says the constraint was relaxed beginning with version 3.22.0; passage [5] says newer versions can still read such databases as long as conditions are met.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 4,
        "quote": "This constraint was relaxed beginning with SQLite version 3.22.0 (2018-01-22)."
      },
      {
        "index": 5,
        "quote": "On newer versions of SQLite, a WAL-mode database on read-only media, or a WAL-mode database that lacks write permission, can still be read as long as one or more of the following conditions are met:"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "It is still good practice to switch to journal_mode=DELETE before burning a database image onto read-only media.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [6] directly recommends converting the database to PRAGMA journal_mode=DELETE prior to burning an SQLite database image onto read-only media.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 6,
        "quote": "Even though it is possible to open a read-only WAL-mode database, it is good practice to convert the database to PRAGMA journal_mode=DELETE prior to burning an SQLite database image onto read-only media."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Before 3.11.0, rollback journal modes were likely to be faster for transactions over about 100 MB.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [2] states that for transactions larger than about 100 megabytes, traditional rollback journal modes will likely be faster, and the context indicates this was before the 3.11.0 improvement.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 2,
        "quote": "For transactions larger than about 100 megabytes, traditional rollback journal modes will likely be faster."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "From 3.11.0 on WAL handles large transactions as efficiently as rollback mode.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [2] explicitly says that beginning with version 3.11.0, WAL mode works as efficiently with large transactions as rollback mode.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 2,
        "quote": "Beginning with version 3.11.0 (2016-02-15), WAL mode works as efficiently with large transactions as does rollback mode."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "Since version 3.7.4, a process can even use WAL without shared memory, as long as it sets the locking mode to EXCLUSIVE before its first access.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [8] states that beginning in version 3.7.4, WAL databases can be used even if shared memory is unavailable as long as locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 8,
        "quote": "Beginning in SQLite version 3.7.4 (2010-12-07), WAL databases can be created, read, and written even if shared memory is unavailable as long as the locking_mode is set to EXCLUSIVE before the first attempted access."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "In WAL mode the file format version numbers in the database header are raised from 1 to 2.",
    "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [9] explicitly says the database file format version numbers are increased from 1 to 2 in WAL mode.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 9,
        "quote": "the database file format version numbers (bytes 18 and 19 in the database header) are increased from 1 to 2 in WAL mode."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "SQLite versions older than 3.22.0 report \"file is encrypted or is not a database\" when they open a WAL-mode database.",
    "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
    "reasoning": "Passage [9] says this error is for older versions prior to version 3.7.0, not all versions older than 3.22.0.",
    "citations": [
      {
        "index": 9,
        "quote": "To prevent older versions of SQLite (prior to version 3.7.0, 2010-07-22) from trying to recover a WAL-mode database (and making matters worse) the database file format version numbers (bytes 18 and 19 in the database header) are increased from 1 to 2 in WAL mode."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "claim_text": "The 3.22.0 release also made WAL the default journal mode for newly created databases.",
    "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
    "reasoning": "No passage states that version 3.22.0 made WAL the default journal mode for newly created databases.",
    "citations": []
  }
]}

{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim_text": "For most production deployments, the safest answer is to enable both persistence methods.",
      "verdict": "UNSUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passages recommend using both methods for PostgreSQL-level data safety, but do not state that this is the safest choice for most production deployments.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Using both persistence methods gives a degree of data safety comparable to what PostgreSQL provides.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly says to use both methods for PostgreSQL-comparable data safety.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The general indication you should use both persistence methods is if you want a degree of data safety comparable to what PostgreSQL can provide you."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB is a compact, single-file point-in-time representation of Redis data.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage describes RDB exactly in these terms.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "RDB is a very compact single-file point-in-time representation of your Redis data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB is ideal for backups and disaster recovery.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says RDB files are perfect for backups and very good for disaster recovery.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "RDB files are perfect for backups."
        },
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "RDB is very good for disaster recovery, being a single compact file that can be transferred to far data centers, or onto Amazon S3 (possibly encrypted)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "You can archive hourly RDB snapshots for 24 hours and daily snapshots for 30 days.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage gives this exact example of RDB backup archiving.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "For instance you may want to archive your RDB files every hour for the latest 24 hours, and to save an RDB snapshot every day for 30 days."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB allows faster restarts with big datasets compared to AOF.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states this advantage of RDB.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "RDB allows faster restarts with big datasets compared to AOF."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB minimizes performance impact because the parent process only forks a child and never performs disk I/O.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage describes the parent process forking a child for persistence and never doing disk I/O.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "the only work the Redis parent process needs to do in order to persist is forking a child that will do all the rest. The parent process will never perform disk I/O or alike."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB snapshots are usually taken every five minutes or more, so a crash without clean shutdown can lose the latest minutes of data.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says snapshots are usually created every five minutes or more, so the latest minutes of data may be lost after an unclean shutdown.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "However you'll usually create an RDB snapshot every five minutes or more, so in case of Redis stopping working without a correct shutdown for any reason you should be prepared to lose the latest minutes of data."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB's main weakness is durability.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says RDB is not good if you need to minimize data loss, aligning with the durability weakness claim.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "RDB is NOT good if you need to minimize the chance of data loss in case Redis stops working (for example after a power outage)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Frequent fork() in RDB can stall the server for milliseconds or even a second on very large datasets with weak CPUs.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states fork() can be time consuming and may stop Redis serving clients for milliseconds to one second on very large datasets with poor CPU performance.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "fork() can be time consuming if the dataset is big, and may result in Redis stopping serving clients for some milliseconds or even for one second if the dataset is very big and the CPU performance is not great."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF is much more durable than RDB.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage directly says AOF is much more durable.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Using AOF Redis is much more durable"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With the default fsync-every-second policy, AOF loses at most one second of writes and write performance is still great.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says the default fsync every second policy loses at most one second of writes while performance is still great.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "With the default policy of fsync every second, write performance is still great. fsync is performed using a background thread and the main thread will try hard to perform writes when no fsync is in progress, so you can only lose one second worth of writes."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF's append-only log resists corruption from power outages.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says append-only log has no corruption problems in a power outage.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "The AOF log is an append-only log, so there are no seeks, nor corruption problems if there is a power outage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A half-written AOF command can be fixed with redis-check-aof.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says redis-check-aof can easily fix a log ending with a half-written command.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "Even if the log ends with a half-written command for some reason (disk full or other reasons) the redis-check-aof tool is able to fix it easily."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF can recover from an accidental FLUSHALL by removing the latest command from the log.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage supports this recovery procedure, with the condition that no AOF rewrite has occurred in the meantime.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "even if you've accidentally flushed everything using the `FLUSHALL` command, as long as no rewrite of the log was performed in the meantime, you can still save your data set just by stopping the server, removing the latest command, and restarting Redis again."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF files are usually bigger than equivalent RDB files for the same dataset.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage explicitly states this trade-off.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "AOF files are usually bigger than the equivalent RDB files for the same dataset."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF can be slower than RDB depending on the fsync policy.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says AOF can be slower depending on the exact fsync policy.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "AOF can be slower than RDB depending on the exact fsync policy."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "With fsync disabled, AOF matches RDB speed even under high load.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says with fsync disabled AOF should be exactly as fast as RDB even under high load.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "with fsync disabled it should be exactly as fast as RDB even under high load."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB provides more guarantees about maximum latency under huge write load.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says RDB provides more guarantees about maximum latency even under a huge write load.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 3,
          "quote": "Still RDB is able to provide more guarantees about the maximum latency even in the case of a huge write load."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "RDB alone is a reasonable choice if you care about data but can tolerate losing a few minutes in a disaster.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says you can simply use RDB alone if you can live with a few minutes of data loss in disasters.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "If you care a lot about your data, but still can live with a few minutes of data loss in case of disasters, you can simply use RDB alone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "AOF alone is discouraged.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage says the authors discourage using AOF alone.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "There are many users using AOF alone, but we discourage it"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Periodic RDB snapshots are valuable for backups, faster restarts, and protection against bugs in the AOF engine.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage lists these reasons for occasionally having an RDB snapshot even when using AOF.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "since to have an RDB snapshot from time to time is a great idea for doing database backups, for faster restarts, and in the event of bugs in the AOF engine."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "When both AOF and RDB are enabled, Redis uses the AOF file on restart to reconstruct the dataset because it is most complete.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "The passage states that the AOF file is used on restart and is guaranteed to be the most complete.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 7,
          "quote": "In the case both AOF and RDB persistence are enabled and Redis restarts the AOF file will be used to reconstruct the original dataset since it is guaranteed to be the most complete."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
